+# int8, symmetric, one bf16 scale per (output column, group of 128 inputs along K): w ~ s[n, g] * q[n, k], q in
+# -127..127. 8.125 bits a weight (sfxnz's INT8 g128). On GLM-5.3-Flash's own weights (zai-org/GLM-5.3-Flash, 12
+# matrices of every non-expert class, docs/QUALITY-PLAN.md section 1) the relative weight error is 0.65-0.73%
+# (eh_proj 1.9%), against 8.6-9.4% for q4mse; per output channel it is 0.65-1.25% (eh_proj 14.7%: its [embedding |
+# hidden] halves have different scales); a zero-point buys 5-10% less error for 1.5% more bytes and an extra
+# group-sum term. The scale is the smallest bf16 >= amax / 127 (nothing clips); an all-zero group gets scale 1.
+#
+# Arithmetic (every kernel, decode and prefill): for group g (G = 128 inputs; 64 when K is not a multiple of 128)
+#     P[m, n, g] = x[m, g-block] . q[n, g-block]      tensor cores, bf16 x integer-valued bf16 (exact) -> fp32, one
+#                                                     mma chain from zero in 64-input halves (``tl.dot(.., .., P)``)
+#     y[m, n]    = sum over g, in order, of  P[m, n, g] * s[n, g]      (one FMA a group)
+# with the K groups split into ``q8_split_k`` slices fixed by the shape (qmm's slices, so the callers' split-K
+# partial buffers fit) and added in slice order: a row's bits never depend on the row tile or on the other rows, and
+# drafted == serial / batched == alone hold as for Q4. No group sums (xs) are read. The weights are read as int32
+# words (``_q8_unpack``) so Triton streams them with cp.async.
+Q8_GS = 128
